+++
!!Mari!! ([[ Cuneiform ]]: 𒈠𒌷𒆠, //ma-ri^^ki^^//, modern !!Tell Hariri!!;
Arabic: تل حريري) was an ancient [[ Semitic | semitic-people ]]
[[ city-state ]] in modern-day [[ Syria ]]. Its remains form a
[[ tell | tell-archaeology ]] 11 kilometres (6.8 mi) north-west of
[[ Abu Kamal ]] on the western bank of the [[ Euphrates River | euphrates ]],
some 120 kilometres (75 mi) southeast of [[ Deir ez-Zor ]]. It flourished as a
trade center and hegemonic state between 2900 BC and 1759 BC.[[ ^1 ]] The city
was built in the middle of the Euphrates trade routes between [[ Sumer ]] in
the south and the [[ Eblaite kingdom | ebla ]] and the [[ Levant ]] in the
west.

Mari was first abandoned in the middle of the 26th century BC but was rebuilt
and became the capital of a hegemonic
[[ East Semitic | east-semitic-languages ]] state before 2500 BC. This second
Mari engaged in a long war with its rival [[ Ebla ]] and is known for its
strong affinity with Sumerian culture. It was destroyed in the 23rd century BC
by the [[ Akkadians ]], who allowed the city to be rebuilt and appointed a
military governor (//[[ Shakkanakku ]]//). The governors became independent
with the disintegration of the Akkadian Empire, and rebuilt the city as a
regional center of the Euphrates valley. The Shakkanakkus ruled Mari until the
second half of the 19th century BC, when the dynasty collapsed for unknown
reasons. A short time later, Mari became the capital of the [[ Amorite ]] Lim
dynasty. The Amorite Mari lasted only a short time before it was destroyed by
[[ Babylonia ]] in c. 1761 BC, but it survived as a small settlement under the
rule of the Babylonians and the [[ Assyrians | assyria ]] before being
abandoned and forgotten during the [[ Hellenistic period ]].

The Mariotes worshiped both
[[ Semitic and Sumerian deities | list-of-mesopotamian-deities ]] and
established their city as a major trading center. Although the pre-Amorite
periods were characterized by heavy Sumerian cultural influence, Mari was not
a city of Sumerian immigrants but a Semitic-speaking nation with a dialect
similar to [[ Eblaite | eblaite-language ]]. The Amorites were
[[ West Semites | west-semitic-languages ]] who began to settle the area
before the 21st century BC; by the Lim dynasty (c. 1830 BC), they became the
dominant population in the [[ Fertile Crescent ]].

Mari's discovery in 1933 provided an important insight into the geopolitical
map of ancient [[ Mesopotamia ]] and [[ Syria | syria-region ]], due to the
discovery of more than 25,000 tablets explicating the state administration in
the 2nd millennium BC and the nature of diplomatic relations among the
political powers of the region. They also revealed the wide trading networks
of the 18th century BC, which connected areas as far as [[ Afghanistan ]] in
[[ Southern Asia | south-asia ]] and [[ Crete ]] in the
[[ Mediterranean | mediterranean-basin ]].

Based on remote sensing, between 2011 and 2022 the site was subject to
extensive looting and is now pockmarked by looting holes.[[ ^1 ]]

== Name

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Mari-ki_in_cuneiform_on_the_statue_of_Iddi-Ilum.jpg/250px-Mari-ki_in_cuneiform_on_the_statue_of_Iddi-Ilum.jpg" height="315" width="180"

  <figcaption>The name of Mari ([[ Cuneiform ]]: 𒈠𒌷𒆠, //ma-ri^^ki^^//), on the [[ statue of Iddi-Ilum ]], c. 2090 BC

Written in [[ Cuneiform ]] 𒈠𒌷𒆠 (//ma-ri^^ki^^//),[[ ^2 ]] the name of the
city can be traced to [[ Itūr-Mēr ]], an ancient [[ storm deity ]] of northern
[[ Mesopotamia ]] and Syria, who was considered the [[ tutelary deity ]] of
the city,[[ ^3 ]] [[ Georges Dossin ]] noted that the name of the city was
spelled identically to that of the storm god and concluded that Mari was named
after him.[[ ^4 ]]

== History

=== Early Bronze Age

==== First kingdom

Evidence shows that Mari [[ ^5 ]] was founded as a new city in c. 2900 BC
during the Mesopotamian
[[ Early Dynastic period I | early-dynastic-period-mesopotamia#periodization ]],
established to control the waterways of the [[ Euphrates ]] trade routes
connecting the [[ Levant ]] with the [[ Sumerian | sumer ]]
south.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]] The city was built about 1 to 2 kilometres (0.62 to
1.24 mi) from the Euphrates river to protect it from floods,[[ ^5 ]] and was
connected to the river by an artificial [[ canal ]] 7 to 10 kilometres (4.3 to
6.2 mi) long, the route of which has not been identified.[[ ^7 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Mari_landmarks.png/250px-Mari_landmarks.png" height="234" width="250"

  <figcaption>Mari's landmarks

The Mari site is difficult to excavate as it is buried deep under later layers
of habitation.[[ ^6 ]] A circular flood embankment was unearthed,[[ ^6 ]]
containing an area 300 metres (980 ft) in length for gardens and craftsmen's
quarters,[[ ^7 ]] and a defensive
[[ circular internal rampart | circular-rampart ]] 6.7 m (22 ft) thick[[ ^6 ]]
and 8 to 10 metres (26 to 33 ft) high, strengthened by defensive
towers.[[ ^7 ]] Other findings include one of the city gates, a street
beginning at the center and ending at the gate, and residential
houses.[[ ^6 ]] Mari had a central mound,[[ ^8 ]] but no temple or palace has
been unearthed there.[[ ^6 ]] A large building was however excavated (with
dimensions of 32 m x 25 m), seemingly with an administrative function. It had
stone foundations and rooms up to 12 metres (39 ft) long and 6 metres (20 ft)
wide.[[ ^9 ]] The city was abandoned c. 2550 BC at the end of the
[[ Early Dynastic period II | early-dynastic-period-mesopotamia#periodization ]],
for unknown reasons.[[ ^6 ]]

==== Second kingdom

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ancient_Near_East_2400BC.svg/500px-Ancient_Near_East_2400BC.svg.png" height="244" width="340"

  <figcaption>Mari on the map of Ancient Orient around 2400 BCE. Conflict between Ebla and Mari.

Near the beginning of
[[ Early Dynastic period III | early-dynastic-period-mesopotamia#periodization ]],
before 2500 BC, [[ ^10 ]] Mari was rebuilt and populated
again.[[ ^6 ]][[ ^11 ]] The new city kept many of the first city's exterior
features, including the internal rampart and gate.[[ ^6 ]][[ ^12 ]] Also kept
was the outer circular embankment measuring 1.9 km (1.2 mi) in diameter, which
was topped by a wall two meters thick capable of[[ ^12 ]] protecting
[[ archers | archery ]].[[ ^6 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Ebish-Il_Louvre_AO17551.jpg/250px-Ebish-Il_Louvre_AO17551.jpg" height="376" width="250"

  <figcaption
    [[ Statue of Ebih-Il ]], a superintendent in Mari. (25th century BC)

The new Mari was carefully planned. Its internal urban structure was radically
different from the preceding incarnation.[[ ^13 ]] First to be built were the
streets that descended from the elevated center into the gates which ensured
the drainage of rainwater.[[ ^6 ]]

A structure known as the [[ Royal Palace of Mari ]] was built in the heart of
the city; the palace also served as a temple.[[ ^6 ]] Four successive
architectural levels from the second kingdom's palace have been unearthed (the
oldest is designated //P3//, while the latest is //P0//). The last two levels
are dated to the [[ Akkadian period | history-of-sumer#akkadian-empire ]] of
Sumer.[[ ^14 ]] The first two levels were excavated;[[ ^14 ]] the findings
include a temple dubbed the Enceinte Sacrée ([[ sacred enclosure ]]) dedicated
to an unknown deity,[[ ^14 ]][[ ^15 ]] a columned throne room, and a hall with
three double wood pillars leading to the temple.[[ ^14 ]]

Six smaller temples were also discovered, including the temple called the
Massif Rouge (also dedicated to an unknown deity), and temples dedicated to
Ninni-Zaza (INANA.ZA.ZA),[[ ^16 ]] [[ Ishtarat ]],[[ ^17 ]] [[ Ishtar ]],
[[ Ninhursag ]], and [[ Shamash ]].[[ ^15 ]] All the temples except that of
Ishtar were located in the center of the city; the area between the Enceinte
Sacrée and the Massif Rouge is thought to be the administrative center of the
[[ high priest ]].[[ ^15 ]]

The second kingdom appears to have been a powerful and prosperous political
center,[[ ^10 ]] its kings held the title of [[ Lugal ]],[[ ^18 ]] and many
are attested in the city, the most important source being the letter of king
Enna-Dagan c. 2350 BC,[[ ^2 ]][[ ^20 ]] which was sent to [[ Irkab-Damu ]] of
[[ Ebla ]],[[ ^3 ]]. In it, the Mariote king mentions his predecessors and
their military achievements.[[ ^22 ]] However, the reading of this letter is
still uncertain and many interpretations have been presented by
scholars.[[ ^23 ]][[ ^24 ]][[ ^25 ]]

===== Mari–Ebla war

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Helmetted_warrior_with_axe%2C_Mari.jpg/250px-Helmetted_warrior_with_axe%2C_Mari.jpg" height="310" width="190"

  <figcaption>Helmetted warrior with axe, Mari

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Ishqi-Mari_%28dark_background%29.jpg/250px-Ishqi-Mari_%28dark_background%29.jpg" height="334" width="190"

  <figcaption>[[ Ishqi-Mari ]], king of the Second Kingdom of Mari, circa 2300 BC

The earliest attested king in the letter of Enna-Dagan is [[ Ansud ]], who is
mentioned as attacking Ebla, the traditional rival of Mari with whom it had a
long war,[[ ^26 ]] and conquering many of Ebla's cities, including the land of
Belan.[[ ^4 ]][[ ^25 ]] The next king mentioned in the letter is [[ Saʿumu ]],
who conquered the lands of Ra'ak and Nirum.[[ ^5 ]][[ ^25 ]] King
[[ Kun-Damu ]] of Ebla defeated Mari in the middle of the 25th century
BC.[[ ^29 ]] The war continued with [[ Išhtup-Išar ]] of Mari's conquest of
[[ Emar ]][[ ^25 ]] at a time of Eblaite weakness in the mid-24th century BC.
King [[ Igrish-Halam ]] of Ebla had to pay tribute to [[ Iblul-Il ]] of
Mari,[[ ^29 ]][[ ^30 ]] who is mentioned in the letter, conquering many of
Ebla's cities and campaigning in the [[ Burman | sweyhat ]] region.[[ ^25 ]]

Enna-Dagan also received tribute;[[ ^30 ]] his reign fell entirely within the
reign of [[ Irkab-Damu ]] of Ebla,[[ ^31 ]] who managed to defeat Mari and end
the tribute.[[ ^21 ]] Mari defeated Ebla's ally
[[ Nagar | tell-brak#kingdom-of-nagar ]] in year seven of the Eblaite
[[ vizier ]] [[ Ibrium ]]'s term, causing the blockage of trade routes between
Ebla and southern Mesopotamia through upper Mesopotamia.[[ ^32 ]] The war
reached a climax when the Eblaite vizier [[ Ibbi-Sipish ]] made an alliance
with Nagar and [[ Kish | kish-sumer ]] to defeat Mari in a battle near
[[ Terqa ]].[[ ^33 ]] Ebla itself suffered its first destruction a few years
after Terqa in c. 2300 BC,[[ ^34 ]] during the reign of the Mariote king
[[ Hidar ]].[[ ^35 ]] According to Alfonso Archi, Hidar was succeeded by
[[ Ishqi-Mari ]] whose royal seal was discovered. It depicts battle scenes,
causing Archi to suggest that he was responsible for the destruction of Ebla
while still a general.[[ ^35 ]][[ ^36 ]]

===== Destruction of Mari by Sargon of Akkad

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Soldiers%2C_shell_inlay%2C_Mari%2C_Syria.jpg/250px-Soldiers%2C_shell_inlay%2C_Mari%2C_Syria.jpg" height="141" width="250"

  <figcaption>Soldiers, shell inlay. Mari

Just a decade after Ebla's destruction (c. 2300 BC middle chronology), Mari
itself was destroyed and burned by [[ Sargon of Akkad ]], as shown by one of
his year names ("//Year in which Mari was
destroyed//").[[ ^37 ]][[ ^38 ]][[ ^39 ]][[ ^33 ]] [[ Michael Astour ]]
proposed the date as c. 2265 BC ([[ short chronology ]]).[[ ^40 ]]
[[ Ishqi-Mari ]] was probably the last king of Mari before the conquests by
the [[ Akkadian Empire ]].[[ ^41 ]] [[ Sargon of Akkad ]] collected tribute
from Mari and [[ Elam ]]:[[ ^42 ]]

<blockquote
  Sargon the King bowed down to [[ Dagan | dagon ]] in [[ Tuttul ]]. He (Dagan)
  gave to him (Sargon) the Upper Land: Mari, Iarmuti, and [[ Ebla ]], as far as
  the Cedar Forest and the Silver Mountains

— Nippur inscription of Sargon[[ ^43 ]][[ ^44 ]]

==== Third kingdom

Mari was deserted for two generations before being restored by the Akkadian
king [[ Manishtushu ]].[[ ^45 ]] A governor was appointed to govern the city
who held the title [[ Shakkanakku ]] (military governor).[[ ^46 ]] Akkad kept
direct control over the city, which is evident by [[ Naram-Sin of Akkad ]]'s
appointment of two of his daughters to priestly offices in the city.[[ ^46 ]]

===== Shakkanakku dynasty

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Statue_of_Puzur-Ishtar.jpg/250px-Statue_of_Puzur-Ishtar.jpg" height="333" width="190"

  <figcaption>[[ Puzur-Ishtar ]], [[ Shakkanakku ]] (Military Governor) of Mari, circa 2050 BC

In the Akkadian period, the first member of the [[ Shakkanakku ]] dynasty on
the lists is Ididish, who was appointed in c. 2266 BC.[[ ^6 ]][[ ^48 ]]
According to the lists, Ididish ruled for 60 years[[ ^49 ]] and was succeeded
by his son=, making the position hereditary.[[ ^50 ]]

The layout of the third Mari was similar to that of its predecessor;[[ ^51 ]]
phase //P0// of the old royal palace was replaced by a new palace for the
Shakkanakku.[[ ^52 ]] Another smaller palace was built in the eastern part of
the city[[ ^8 ]] housing royal burials that date to the former
periods.[[ ^53 ]] The ramparts were rebuilt and strengthened while the
embankment was turned into a defensive wall that reached 10 metres (33 ft) in
width.[[ ^52 ]] The former sacred enclosure was maintained,[[ ^52 ]] so was
the temple of Ninhursag. However, the temples of Ninni-Zaza and Ishtarat
disappeared,[[ ^52 ]] while a new temple called the "temple of lions"
(dedicated to [[ Dagan | dagon ]]),[[ ^54 ]] was built by the Shakkanakku
[[ Ishtup-Ilum ]] and attached to it, was a rectangular terrace that measured
40 by 20 metres (131 ft × 66 ft) for sacrifices.[[ ^52 ]][[ ^8 ]][[ ^55 ]]

Akkad disintegrated during [[ Shar-Kali-Sharri ]]'s reign,[[ ^56 ]] and Mari
gained its independence, but the use of the Shakkanakku title continued during
the following [[ Third Dynasty of Ur ]] period.[[ ^57 ]] A princess of Mari
married the son of king [[ Ur-Nammu ]] of [[ Ur ]],[[ ^58 ]][[ ^59 ]] and Mari
was nominally under Ur hegemony.[[ ^60 ]] However, the
[[ vassalage | vassal ]] did not impede the independence of
Mari,[[ ^61 ]][[ ^62 ]] and some Shakkanakkus used the royal title [[ Lugal ]]
in their votive inscriptions, while using the title of Shakkanakku in their
correspondence with the Ur's court.[[ ^63 ]] The dynasty ended for unknown
reasons not long before the establishment of the next dynasty, which took
place in the second half of the 19th century BC.[[ ^64 ]][[ ^65 ]][[ ^66 ]]

=== Middle Bronze Age

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Third_Mari.png/330px-Third_Mari.png" height="247" width="320"

  <figcaption>Mari at its height in the eighteenth century BC

==== Lim dynasty

The second millennium BC in the [[ Fertile Crescent ]] was characterized by
the expansion of the [[ Amorites ]], which culminated with them dominating and
ruling most of the region,[[ ^67 ]] including Mari which in c. 1830 BC, became
the seat of the Amorite Lim dynasty under king
[[ Yaggid-Lim ]].[[ ^66 ]][[ ^68 ]] However, the
[[ epigraphical | epigraphy ]] and archaeological evidences showed a high
degree of continuity between the Shakkanakku and the Amorite
eras.[[ ^7 ]][[ ^58 ]]

Yaggid-Lim was the ruler of Suprum before establishing himself in
Mari,[[ ^8 ]][[ ^9 ]][[ ^71 ]] he entered an alliance with [[ Ila-kabkabu ]]
of [[ Ekallatum ]], but the relations between the two monarchs changed to an
open war.[[ ^70 ]][[ ^72 ]] The conflict ended with Ila-kabkabu capturing
Yaggid-Lim's heir [[ Yahdun-Lim ]] and according to a tablet found in Mari,
Yaggid-Lim who survived Ila-kabkabu was killed by his
servants.[[ ^10 ]][[ ^70 ]] However, in c. 1820 BC Yahdun-Lim was firmly in
control as king of Mari.[[ ^11 ]][[ ^72 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Goddess_of_the_vase%2C_Mari%2C18th_century_BCE.jpg/250px-Goddess_of_the_vase%2C_Mari%2C18th_century_BCE.jpg" height="345" width="190"

  <figcaption>Goddess of the vase, Mari, 18th century BC

Yahdun-Lim started his reign by subduing seven of his rebelling tribal
leaders, and rebuilding the walls of Mari and Terqa in addition to building a
new fort which he named Dur-Yahdun-Lim.[[ ^74 ]] He then expanded west and
claimed to have reached the
[[ Mediterranean | mediterranean-sea ]],[[ ^75 ]][[ ^76 ]] however he later
had to face a rebellion by the Yaminite nomads who were centered at
[[ Tuttul ]], and the rebels were supported by [[ Yamhad ]]'s king
[[ Sumu-Epuh ]], whose interests were threatened by the recently established
alliance between Yahdun-Lim and [[ Eshnunna ]].[[ ^61 ]][[ ^75 ]] Yahdun-Lim
defeated the Yaminites but an open war with Yamhad was avoided,[[ ^77 ]] as
the Mariote king became occupied by his rivalry with [[ Shamshi-Adad I ]] of
[[ Shubat-Enlil ]], the son of the late Ila-kabkabu.[[ ^78 ]] The war ended in
a defeat for Mari,[[ ^78 ]][[ ^79 ]] and Yahdun-Lim was assassinated in c.
1798 BC by his possible son Sumu-Yamam,[[ ^80 ]][[ ^81 ]] who himself got
assassinated two years after ascending the throne while Shamshi-Adad advanced
and annexed Mari.[[ ^82 ]]

==== Shamshi-Adad and Yasmah-Adad

Shamshi-Adad (r. 1809-1775 BC), ruler of the
[[ Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia ]], appointed his son [[ Yasmah-Adad ]] on the
throne of Mari, the new king married Yahdun-Lim's daughter,[[ ^83 ]][[ ^84 ]]
while the rest of the Lim family took refuge in Yamhad,[[ ^85 ]] and the
annexation was officially justified by what Shamshi-Adad considered sinful
acts committed by the Lim family.[[ ^86 ]] To strengthen his position against
his new enemy Yamhad, Shamshi-Adad married Yasmah-Adad to Betlum, the daughter
of [[ Ishi-Addu ]] of [[ Qatna ]].[[ ^84 ]] However, Yasmah-Adad neglected his
bride causing a crisis with Qatna, and he proved to be an unable leader
causing the rage of his father who died in c. 1776
BC,[[ ^84 ]][[ ^87 ]][[ ^88 ]] while the armies of [[ Yarim-Lim I ]] of Yamhad
were advancing in support of [[ Zimri-Lim ]], the heir of the Lim
dynasty.[[ ^12 ]][[ ^88 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Investiture_of_Zimri-Lim_Louvre_AO19826_n01.jpg/250px-Investiture_of_Zimri-Lim_Louvre_AO19826_n01.jpg" height="167" width="250"

  <figcaption>[[ Investiture of Zimri-Lim ]] (18th century BC)

==== Zimri-Lim of Mari

After the death of Shamshi-Adad, as Zimri-Lim advanced, a leader of the
//Sim'alites// (Zimri-Lim's tribe) overthrew Yasmah-Adad,[[ ^90 ]] opening the
road for Zimri-Lim who arrived a few months after Yasmah-Adad's
escape,[[ ^91 ]] and married princess [[ Shibtu ]], a daughter of Yarim-Lim I,
a short time after his enthronement in c. 1776 BC.[[ ^88 ]] Zimri-Lim's
ascension to the throne with the help of Yarim-Lim I affected Mari's status,
Zimri-Lim referred to Yarim-Lim as his father, and the Yamhadite king was able
to order Mari as the mediator between Yamhad's main deity [[ Hadad ]] and
Zimri-Lim, who declared himself a servant of Hadad.[[ ^92 ]]

Zimri-Lim started his reign with a campaign against the //Yaminites//, he also
established alliances with [[ Eshnunna ]] and [[ Hammurabi ]] of
[[ Babylon ]],[[ ^85 ]] and sent his armies to aid the Babylonians.[[ ^93 ]]
The new king directed his expansion policy toward the north in the
[[ Upper Khabur | khabur-euphrates ]] region, which was named
Idamaraz,[[ ^94 ]] where he subjugated the local petty kingdoms in the region
such as [[ Urkesh ]],[[ ^95 ]] and Talhayum, forcing them into
vassalage.[[ ^96 ]] The expansion was met by the resistance of
[[ Qarni-Lim | andarig ]], the king of [[ Andarig ]],[[ ^97 ]] whom Zimri-Lim
defeated, securing the Mariote control over the region in c. 1771 BC,[[ ^98 ]]
and the kingdom prospered as a trading center and entered a period of relative
peace.[[ ^88 ]] Zimri-Lim's greatest heritage was the renovation of the
[[ Royal Palace | royal-palace-of-mari ]], which was expanded greatly to
contain 275 rooms,[[ ^8 ]][[ ^99 ]] exquisite artifacts such as //The Goddess
of the Vase// statue,[[ ^100 ]] and a royal archive that contained thousands
of tablets.[[ ^101 ]]

==== Babylonian period

Mari's relations with Babylon worsened with a dispute over the city of
[[ Hīt ]] that consumed much time in negotiations,[[ ^102 ]] during which a
war against [[ Elam ]] involved both kingdoms in c. 1765 BC.[[ ^103 ]] Babylon
invaded in c. 1761 BC under the rule of [[ Hammurabi ]] and defeated
Zimri-Lin, ending the Lim dynasty,[[ ^104 ]] while Terqa became the capital of
a rump state called the [[ Kingdom of Ḫana ]].[[ ^105 ]] In the south, the
region of [[ Suhum ]] became a Babylonian province.

Mari survived the destruction and rebelled against Babylon in c. 1759 BC,
which prompted Hammurabi to raze the city.[[ ^106 ]] Marc Van De Mieroop
suggests that Hammurab, in a gesture of mercy, may have allowed Mari to
survive as a small village under Babylonian administration.[[ ^106 ]]

=== Late Bronze Age

Mari became part of [[ Assyria | assyria#middle-assyrian-empire-13921056-bc ]]
and was listed among the territories conquered by the Assyrian king
[[ Tukulti-Ninurta I ]] (r. 1243–1207 BC).[[ ^107 ]] Mari frequently changed
hands between Assyria and Babylon.[[ ^107 ]]

=== Iron Age

In the middle of the eleventh century BC, Mari became part of Hana. The king
of Hana Tukulti-Mer assumed the title //king of Mari// and rebelled against
Assyria, prompting Assyrian king [[ Ashur-bel-kala ]] (r. 1074–1056 BC) to
attack.[[ ^107 ]] In the first half of the 8th century BC Mari came firmly
under the authority of the [[ Neo-Assyrian Empire ]]. It was assigned to the
governorship of a certain Nergal-Erish, under the authority of king
[[ Adad-Nirari III ]] (r. 810–783 BC).[[ ^107 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Shamsh-res-usur%2C_governor_of_Mari_and_Suhi.jpg/250px-Shamsh-res-usur%2C_governor_of_Mari_and_Suhi.jpg" height="214" width="250"

  <figcaption>Shamash-Risha-Usur (c. 760 BC)

In c. 760 BC Shamash-Risha-Usur,[[ ^108 ]] an autonomous administrator under
the nominal authority of [[ Ashur-dan III ]], ruled parts of the upper middle
Euphrates; he styled himself the governor of the lands of [[ Suhu ]] and Mari,
as did his son Ninurta-Kudurri-Usur.[[ ^107 ]] In 760 BC Mari was part of
Laqe,[[ ^13 ]], suggesting that the title "governor" was a historical
designation.[[ ^107 ]]

The city of Mari persisted as a small settlement until the
[[ Hellenistic period ]] (323–30 BC) when it disappeared from
records.[[ ^107 ]]

=== Modern history

By 2015, the [[ Islamic State ]] (ISIS) had devastated and systematically
looted the site, particularly the [[ Royal Palace of Mari ]].[[ ^110 ]] It was
one of the first archaeological sites to be occupied by this group.[[ ^111 ]]

== People, language and government

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Bust_orant_Louvre_AO17557.jpg/250px-Bust_orant_Louvre_AO17557.jpg" height="378" width="250"

  <figcaption>A Mariote from the second kingdom. (25th century BC)

Mari's founders were either Sumerians or speakers of an
[[ East Semitic | east-semitic-languages ]] language from [[ Terqa ]] in the
north.[[ ^5 ]] [[ Ignace Gelb ]] relates Mari's foundation with the proposed
[[ Kish civilization ]],[[ ^112 ]] a cultural entity of East Semitic-speaking
populations that stretched from the center of Mesopotamia to Ebla in the
western Levant.[[ ^113 ]]

The population of the second Mari was around 40,000 at its height.[[ ^114 ]]
The population spoke an East Semitic dialect very similar to the
[[ Eblaite language ]]. [[ ^11 ]][[ ^115 ]] In the Shakkanakku period, the
population spoke [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]], also an East Semitic
language.[[ ^116 ]] [[ West Semitic | west-semitic-languages ]] names started
to be attested in Mari from the second kingdom era,[[ ^117 ]] and by the
middle [[ Bronze-Age | bronze-age#near-east-timeline ]], the west Semitic
[[ Amorite ]] tribes became the majority of the pastoral groups in the middle
Euphrates and [[ Khabur | khabur-euphrates ]] valleys.[[ ^118 ]] Amorite names
were in use by end of the Shakkanakku period, including by the ruling
dynasts.[[ ^119 ]]

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Statues_from_Mari._Syria_-_Aleppo_-_National_Museum.jpg/250px-Statues_from_Mari._Syria_-_Aleppo_-_National_Museum.jpg" height="188" width="250"

  <figcaption>Statues from Mari in the [[ National Museum of Aleppo ]]

Mari's population had become predominantly Amorite during the Lim era. Textual
evidence shows the continued use of Akkadian names[[ ^14 ]] and, although the
[[ Amorite language ]] became the dominant tongue, Akkadian remained the
language of writing.[[ ^120 ]][[ ^121 ]][[ ^122 ]] The
[[ pastoral | pastoralism ]] Amorites in Mari were called //Haneans//, a
general term referring to [[ nomads | nomad ]].[[ ^123 ]] These Haneans were
split into the //Yaminites// (sons of the south) and //Sim'alites// (sons of
the north), with the ruling house belonging to the //Sim'al//
branch.[[ ^123 ]] The kingdom of Mari was also home to tribes of [[ Suteans ]]
living in Terqa.[[ ^124 ]]

Mari was an [[ absolute monarchy ]]. The king controlled every aspect of
administration, with the aid of [[ scribes | scribe ]] who filled ministerial
roles.[[ ^125 ]][[ ^126 ]] During the Lim era, Mari was divided into four
provinces in addition to the capital. The provincial seats were located at
[[ Terqa ]], [[ Saggarâtum ]], [[ Qaṭṭunān ]], and [[ Tuttul ]]. Each province
had its own [[ bureaucracy ]]; [[ ^126 ]] the state supplied the villagers
with ploughs and other agricultural equipment in return for a share in the
harvest.[[ ^127 ]]

== Culture and religion

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Woman_polos_Louvre_AO17564.jpg/250px-Woman_polos_Louvre_AO17564.jpg" height="294" width="250"

  <figcaption>A Mariote woman. (25th century BC)

The first and second kingdoms were heavily influenced by the Sumerian
south.[[ ^128 ]] The society was led by an urban [[ oligarchy ]],[[ ^129 ]]
and the citizenry was known for elaborate hair and dress
styles.[[ ^130 ]][[ ^131 ]] They used the Eblaite calendar based on a
[[ solar year | tropical-year ]] divided into twelve
months.[[ ^132 ]][[ ^133 ]] Scribes wrote in the [[ Sumerian language ]]; art
and architecture was indistinguishable from that of Sumer.[[ ^134 ]]

Mesopotamia continued to influence Mariote culture during the Amorite
period,[[ ^135 ]] evidenced by the Babylonian scribal style used in the
city.[[ ^136 ]] Mesopotamian influence had lessened since former periods, and
objects such as royal seals show a clear Syrian origin.[[ ^135 ]] The society
was [[ tribal | tribalism ]],[[ ^137 ]] primarily mostly of farmers and nomads
(//Haneans//),[[ ^138 ]] Unlike in Mesopotamia, the Mariote temple played only
a minor role in everyday life as state power was invested in the royal
palace.[[ ^139 ]] Women enjoyed a relative equality to men;[[ ^140 ]] queen
Shibtu ruled in her husband's name while he was away and she had extensive
authority over the highest officials.[[ ^141 ]]

The pantheon of Mari included both Sumerian and Semitic deities.[[ ^142 ]] For
most of Mari's history, [[ Dagan | dagon ]] was the head of the
pantheon[[ ^143 ]] and Mer the patron deity.[[ ^144 ]] Semitic deities
included Ishtar, [[ ^142 ]] [[ Athtar | attar-god ]], [[ ^145 ]] and
[[ Shamash ]], an omniscient solar god and one of the most important of the
pantheon.[[ ^146 ]][[ ^147 ]] Sumerian deities included Ninhursag,[[ ^142 ]]
[[ Dumuzi | tammuz-deity ]],[[ ^148 ]] [[ Enki ]], [[ Anu ]], and
[[ Enlil ]].[[ ^149 ]] [[ Prophecy ]] was important to temple
activity;[[ ^150 ]] prophets participated in religious festivals and gave
council to the king.[[ ^151 ]]

== Economy

The first Mari provided the oldest wheel workshop yet discovered in
Syria,[[ ^152 ]] and was a center of bronze
[[ metallurgy | metallurgy#history ]].[[ ^5 ]] The city also contained
districts devoted to [[ smelting ]], [[ dyeing ]], and pottery
manufacture,[[ ^14 ]] using charcoal brought by river boats from the upper
[[ Khabur | khabur-euphrates ]] and Euphrates area.[[ ^5 ]]

The second kingdom's economy was based on both agriculture and
trade.[[ ^121 ]] It was centralized and directed through a communal
organization,[[ ^121 ]] with grain stored in communal granaries and
distributed according to social status.[[ ^121 ]] The organization also
controlled the animal herds in the kingdom.[[ ^121 ]] Some groups were direct
beneficiaries of the palace instead of the communal organization, including
the metal and textile producers and military officials.[[ ^121 ]] Ebla was an
important trading partner and rival,[[ ^153 ]] Mari's position made it an
important trading center astride the road linking the Levant and
Mesopotamia.[[ ^154 ]]

The Amorite Mari maintained the older aspects of the economy, still largely
based on irrigated agriculture along the Euphrates valley.[[ ^121 ]] The city
remained a trading center for merchants from Babylonia and other
kingdoms,[[ ^155 ]] with goods from the south and east transported on
riverboats bound for the north, northwest and west.[[ ^156 ]] The main trade
was metals and tin from the [[ Iranian Plateau ]] exported west as far as
[[ Crete ]]. Other goods included copper from [[ Cyprus ]], silver from
[[ Anatolia ]], wood from [[ Lebanon ]], gold from
[[ Egypt | ancient-egypt ]], olive oil, wine, and textiles, and even precious
stones from modern [[ Afghanistan ]].[[ ^156 ]]

== Excavations and archive

<figure
  <img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Ishtup-Ilum_excavation_in_Mari%2C_Syria.jpg/250px-Ishtup-Ilum_excavation_in_Mari%2C_Syria.jpg" height="344" width="250"

  <figcaption>Excavations by the archaeological team of [[ André Parrot ]] in 1936. Discovery of the statue of military Governor [[ Ishtup-Ilum ]]

Mari was discovered in 1933, on the eastern flank of Syria, near the Iraqi
border.[[ ^157 ]] A [[ Bedouin ]] tribe was digging through a mound called
Tell Hariri for a gravestone that would be used for a recently deceased
tribesman, when they came across a headless statue.[[ ^157 ]] After the news
reached the [[ French | french-third-republic ]] authorities currently in
[[ control | french-mandate-for-syria-and-the-lebanon ]] of Syria, the report
was investigated, and digging on the site was started on December 14, 1933, by
archaeologists from the [[ Louvre ]] in Paris.[[ ^157 ]] The location of the
fragment was excavated, revealing the temple of Ishtar, which led to the
commencing of the full scale excavations.[[ ^158 ]] Mari was classified by the
archaeologists as the "most westerly outpost of Sumerian culture".[[ ^159 ]]

Since the beginning of excavations, over 25,000 clay tablets in Akkadian
language written in [[ cuneiform | cuneiform-script ]] were
discovered.[[ ^160 ]] Finds from the excavation are on display in the
[[ Louvre ]],[[ ^161 ]] the [[ National Museum of Aleppo ]],[[ ^162 ]] the
[[ National Museum of Damascus ]],[[ ^147 ]] and the [[ Deir ez-Zor Museum ]].
In the latter, the southern [[ façade | investiture-of-zimrilim ]] of the
//Court of the Palms// room from
[[ Zimri-Lim's palace | royal-palace-of-mari ]] has been reconstructed,
including the wall paintings.[[ ^163 ]]

Mari has been excavated in annual campaigns in 1933–1939, 1951–1956, and since
1960.[[ ^164 ]] [[ André Parrot ]] conducted the first 21 seasons up to
1974,[[ ^165 ]] and was followed by Jean-Claude Margueron
(1979–2004),[[ ^166 ]] and Pascal Butterlin (starting in 2005).[[ ^164 ]] A
journal devoted to the site, released in 8 volumes between 1982 and 1997, was
//Mari: Annales de recherches interdisciplinaires//.[[ ^167 ]][[ ^168 ]]
Archaeologists have tried to determine how many layers the site descends,
according to French archaeologist André Parrot, "each time a vertical probe
was commenced in order to trace the site's history down to virgin soil, such
important discoveries were made that horizontal digging had to be
resumed."[[ ^169 ]]

=== Mari tablets

Over 25,000 tablets were found in the burnt library of Zimri-Lim written in
Akkadian[[ ^170 ]] from a period of 50 years between circa 1800 – 1750
BC.[[ ^171 ]] They give information about the kingdom, its customs, and the
names of people who lived during that time.[[ ^68 ]] More than 3000 are
letters, the remainder includes administrative, economic, and judicial
texts.[[ ^172 ]] Almost all the tablets found were dated to the last 50 years
of Mari's independence,[[ ^172 ]] and most have now been published.[[ ^173 ]]
The language of the texts is official [[ Akkadian | akkadian-language ]], but
proper names and hints in syntax show that the common language of Mari's
inhabitants was [[ Northwest Semitic ]].[[ ^174 ]] Six of the tablets found
were in the [[ Hurrian language ]].[[ ^175 ]]

== Current situation

Excavations stopped from 2011 as a result of the [[ Syrian Civil War ]] and
have not restarted.[[ ^176 ]] The site came under the control of armed gangs
and suffered large scale looting. A 2014 official report revealed that robbers
were focusing on the royal palace, the public baths, the temple of Ishtar, and
the temple of Dagan.[[ ^177 ]] Based on satellite imagery, looting continued
until at least 2017.[[ ^178 ]]

